# System of Natural Liberty

*System of Natural Liberties, System of Natural Liberties, System of Natural Liberties*

Composed of the following interpretations of Rawl's second principle of justice:

- the principle of efficiency, and
- equality as careers open to talents.

---

# Careers Open to Talents

*Career Open to Talents*

Requires that positions in society be open to all who are able to fulfill the required functions, with no formal legal barriers to exclude anyone on irrelevant characteristics.

---

# Fair Equality of Opportunity

*Equality of Opportunities, fair equality of opportunities, Fair Equality of Opportunities, Fair Equality of Opportunities*

Those with similar skills and abilities should have a similar chance of attaining some position/reward in society.

---

# Social Contingency

*social contingencies, Social Contingencies, Social Contingencies*

Things that one cannot control which is decided by the social circumstances that one is born into. Ex. family wealth, class background, quality of schooling, etc.

---

# Natural Contingency

*natural contingencies, Natural Contingencies, Natural Contingencies*

Things that one cannot control which is decided by the genetics/random chance. Ex. intelligence, physical strength and health, imagination and creativity, etc.
One unique example that Rawls gives is that of temporal position, i.e., which generation you are born into.

---

# System of Liberal Equality

*System of Liberal Equalities, System of Liberal Equalities, System of Liberal Equalities*

Composed of the following interpretations of Rawl's second principle of justice:
- the principle of efficiency, and
- equality of opportunity.

---

# Difference Principle

*Difference Principles, lexical difference principle, lexical difference principles, Difference Principles, lexical difference principles, Difference Principles, lexical difference principles*

The well-off in society cannot be made more well-off unless it is to the advantage of the least well-off. There is a more precise formulation known as the *lexical difference principle*, whereby for a representative man, first maximize the worst-off, than the new worst-off, and so on, until lastly regard the best-off.

---

# Chain-connected Expectations

(Rawls) Improving the expectations of the worst-off improves all expectations for those less worse-off.

---

# Close-knit Expectations

To lower or improve the expectations of any representative agent is necessarily to lower or improve the expectations of every other representative agent. In other words, it is impossible for any representative's expectations to be changed without at least one other representative's expectations changed.

---

# Perfect Procedural Justice

*perfect procedural justices, Perfect Procedural Justices, Perfect Procedural Justices*

There exists an independent criterion for a just outcome, and a procedure that is guaranteed to produce it. The results are thus predictable.
Ex. one person cuts the cake, and the other gets to pick which slice they want.

---

# Imperfect Procedural Justice

*imperfect procedural justices, Imperfect Procedural Justices, Imperfect Procedural Justices*

There exists an independent criterion for a just outcome, but not a procedure that is guaranteed to produce it. The results are thus unpredictable.
Ex. the criminal justice system (some innocents are sentenced, while some criminals go free).

---

# Pure Procedural Justice

*Pure Procedural Justices, Pure Procedural Justices, Pure Procedural Justices*

There does not exist an independent criterion for a just outcome. In this case, justice is entirely a product of the proper execution of the procedure, not of the outcome. Importantly, the procedure must be carried out.
Ex. gambling.

---

# Principle of Redress

*Principle of Redresses, Principle of Redresses, Principle of Redresses*

The categorical principle that underserved inequalities must be redressed directly. Society must provide more to those with fewer native assets and those born into the less favorable social positions.

---

# Waterbed Effect

*Waterbed Effects, Waterbed Effects, Waterbed Effects*

(Price theory) The theory that large firms can negotiate with suppliers for lower supplier prices, which translates to higher input costs for smaller firms.
See [[The waterbed effect is false]] for a refutation of this theory.

---

# Mexico

*United Mexican States, Mexicos, Mexicos, Mexicos*

- 1982: Debt crisis due to reaching the point where foreign debt outmatched the earnings power of their incomes, which meant that they were not able to repay their debts.
	- Abandon protectionism, since they had to get loans from the IMF.
	- Resulted in the *lost decade*.
		- Meant that spending on social programs decreased during this period.
	- Led to a period of privatization of parastatals, more market oriented integration.
The PRI was weakened due to the weakened, so the National Action Party was able to take power in 2000.
- 1986: Joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT).
	- This was the precursor to the WTO.
- 1994: Salinas signed NAFTA.
	- This meant more environmental regulations that the country had to follow. This is most likely justified via a race to the bottom situation.
	- Led to the Zapatista Rebellion.
- 1994: the Peso Crisis.
Enrique Peña Nieto
- 2013: Liberalized the oil industry, by allowing foreign and private companies to invest in the oil sector (e.g., PEMEX).
	- Attracted greater investment to the industry after a lot of underinvestment.
	- This was somewhat reverse in recent decades.
Mexico city is one of the worst polluted cities in the world both due to the geography and the poor management by the government.
- 1996: beginning of women representation in the government in Mexico.
- 50/50 parity reform (2019): Instituted a quota whereby 50% of every government position from local to national of the government positions need to be reserved for women.

---

# Non-identity Problem

*nonidentity problem, paradox of future individuals, Non-identity Problems, nonidentity problems, Non-identity Problems, nonidentity problems, Non-identity Problems, nonidentity problems*

The paradoxical conclusion that a person cannot have been considered to have been harmed given that they were brought into existence, and that existence is worth having overall. This is because without that harm, the person brought into existence would not have been them, and a low quality existence is better than non-existence.

---

# Monotonic Preferences

*weakly monotonic, strongly monotonic, weakly monotonics, strongly monotonics, weakly monotonics, strongly monotonics, weakly monotonics, strongly monotonics*

- Weak: If the agent prefers all consumption bundles that have more of all goods.
- Strong: If the agent prefers all consumption bundles that have more of at least one good.
These are both usually too strong of an assumption in microeconomic consumer theory.

---

# Local Nonsatiation

*Local Nonsatiations, Local Nonsatiations, Local Nonsatiations*

For any bundle of foods, there is always another bundle of goods arbitrarily close that is strictly preferred to it.

---

# Longitudinal Data

Data collection from the same source repeatedly over time.

---

# Bryd Rule

*Bryd Rules, Bryd Rules, Bryd Rules*

Passed in 1985 to ensures that reconciliation bills are used for budget purposes, and cannot include extraneous purposes.
	- Also, reconciliation bills cannot increase spending or reduce revenues in the period after budget resolution.
	- Needs 60 votes to sustain provisions that violate the rule.

---

# Reconciliation Bill

*reconciliation bills, Reconciliation Bills, Reconciliation Bills*

A special type of congressional budget legislation which bypasses the usual filibuster rules. Can only concern the following three topics:
- changes to the statutory debt limit
- changes in revenues
- changes in mandatory spending
It overrides the cloture rule by allowing the passage of a bill with a simply majority (rather than the 2/3 required to end filibuster normally).

---

# Parliamentarian

*parliamentarians, Parliamentarians, Parliamentarians*

(Congress) A non-partisan expert on legislative procedure.

---

# Entitlements

Federal programs that guarantee benefits to anyone who meets legally defined eligibility criteria, regardless of the total cost.

---

# Disturbance Theory

*disturbance theories, Disturbance Theories, Disturbance Theories*

(Interest groups) A theory of interest groups which states that even the currently unorganized groups are not ignored, because they have the potential to become organized interest groups.

---

# Collective Action Problem

*Collective Action Problems, Collective Action Problems, Collective Action Problems*

A group of people who share a common interest and who all benefit from the interest being achieved, even those who did not contribute to achieving the interest.

---

# Duverger's Law

*Duverger's Laws, Duverger's Laws, Duverger's Laws*

First-past-the-post (SMP) systems tend to produce two-party systems, whereas proportional representation systems tend produce a multiparty system.

---

# Arguendo

*Arguendos, Arguendos, Arguendos*

"For the sake of argument"; accepting some premise to examine their conclusions without admitting that the premises are true.

---

# Gresham's Law

*Gresham's Laws, Gresham's Laws, Gresham's Laws*

"Good money drives out bad money"; the idea that, when the two currencies are accepted as having similar face values by law, the currency with less intrinsic worth tends to be used more than the other currency in exchanges and thus “drive out” the other currency.

---

# Industrial Policy

*Industrial Policies, Industrial Policies, Industrial Policies*

Government led efforts to encourage the development of all parts of the economy, especially in the lack of sufficient investment from the private sector.

---

# Priority Problem

*Priority Problems, Priority Problems, Priority Problems*

(Rawls) How a society deals with competing moral claims.

---

# Principle of Insufficient Reason

*Principle of Insufficient Reasons, Principle of Insufficient Reasons, Principle of Insufficient Reasons*

(Rawls) When you have no information (e.g., in the original position) about a circumstance, assign equal probabilities to all possible outcomes.

---

# Ideal Theory

*Ideal Theories, Ideal Theories, Ideal Theories*

(Rawls) A theory which deals with ideal circumstances, such as strict compliance with the rules of the theory, having a well-ordered society.

---

# Non-ideal Theory

*Non-ideal Theories, Non-ideal Theories, Non-ideal Theories*

(Rawls) Concerns how to adapt a theory for the non-ideal situations in the real world, a big part of which are the principles for meeting injustice.

---

# Categorical Imperative

*Categorical Imperatives, Categorical Imperatives, Categorical Imperatives*

That the validity of a principle is not contingent upon some particular desire or aim, i.e., it is universal.

---

# Thin Theory of the Good

*thin theory, Thin Theory of the Goods, thin theories, Thin Theory of the Goods, thin theories, Thin Theory of the Goods, thin theories*

(Rawls) That the good is the rational long-term plan given relatively favorable circumstances. This is the main reason for people's want of primary goods, since it helps them satisfy this definition of the good.

---

# Economic Calculation

*Economic Calculations, Economic Calculations, Economic Calculations*

Capitalist economies, via price, offer subjective information about the relative value of different things and different uses of things. This allows for such an economy to efficiently allocate resources. Socialist economies, lacking a price system, fail to achieve this.

---

# Last Great War of Antiquity

*Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628, Last Great War of Antiquities, Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628s, Last Great War of Antiquities, Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628s, Last Great War of Antiquities, Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628s*

The final conflict between the Byzantine Empire and the Sassanid empire, which greatly weakened both sides as to have allowed the rise of the Rashidun.

---

# Teleological

*teleology, Teleologicals, teleologies, Teleologicals, teleologies, Teleologicals, teleologies*

(General) Exploring causality through ends, goals, or purposes.

(Rawls) Defining good irrespective of what is right, then defining right as being whatever produces the most of that good.
Ex. Hedonism, Utilitarianism, Eudaimonism, etc.

---

# Dominant-end

*dominant-end theory, dominant-ends theory, dominant-ends, value monism, eonistic practical deliberation, summum bonum, maximizing conception of rationality, dominant-end theories, dominant-ends theories, value monisms, eonistic practical deliberations, summum bonums, maximizing conception of rationalities, Dominant-ends, dominant-end theories, dominant-ends theories, value monisms, eonistic practical deliberations, summum bonums, maximizing conception of rationalities, Dominant-ends, dominant-end theories, dominant-ends theories, value monisms, eonistic practical deliberations, summum bonums, maximizing conception of rationalities*

(Rawls) Proposes that rational choice requires identifying a single overriding end to which all other ends are subordinate, and then treating all decisions as questions of how best to advance that dominant end.

---

# Congruence Problem

*Congruence Problems, Congruence Problems, Congruence Problems*

(Rawls) Whether it makes sense for a rational person to regard the principles of justice as regulative of their plan of life.

---

# Hyperobject

*hyper-object, Hyperobjects, hyper-objects, Hyperobjects, hyper-objects, Hyperobjects, hyper-objects*

(Morton; Ontology) A metaphysical structure so massive that a single human struggles to understand it.
Ex. the banking system, the government, etc.

---

# Circumvallation

*circumvallations, Circumvallations, Circumvallations*

A defense system built by besiegers facing the besieged.

---

# Contravallation

*Contravallations, Contravallations*

A defense system built by besiegers facing away from the besieged to protect themselves from forces allied to the besieged.

---

# Classical Theory of Interest

*Classical Theory of Interests*

Bases interest rates on real factors in the economy.

---

# Liquidity Preference Theory

Bases interests rates on the supply of money in the economy.